  "framework": "oecd",
  "questions": {
    "analysis": "Questioning emphasized recall and identification (how many parts, what is numerator/denominator). There were occasional higher-level prompts (deciding which of two fractions is a proper fraction), but most questions were closed and choral which limited diagnostic information about individual reasoning.",
    "examples": [
      "Open: \"Why is 3/7 a proper fraction?\"",
      "Closed: \"How many parts do you see?\""
    ],
    "open_ended_count": 4,
    "closed_ended_count": 30
  },
  "strengths": [
    {
      "title": "Clear use of visual models",
      "impact": "Students could map abstract fraction notation (numerator/denominator) to concrete shaded parts, improving comprehension and recall.",
      "analysis": "Visual, concrete representation of fractions supports conceptual understanding for diverse learners and aligns with best practice in early fraction instruction.",
      "evidence": "Part 1 - What happened: E2E Driver used drawn shapes (circles, rectangles, squares), shading and color changes to demonstrate partitioning and to connect numerator/denominator to visible shaded parts. Students counted aloud and pointed to shaded segments while responding, showing the visuals supported comprehension.\nPart 2 - Quote: \"I will show you a model — tell me how many parts there are?\" \"Count the shaded parts and the total parts.\""
    },
    {
      "title": "Strong real-world connection",
      "impact": "Students engaged verbally and were more willing to count and answer; real-world framing likely aided retention and motivation.",
      "analysis": "Linking math to everyday experiences increases relevance and student engagement, making the abstract idea of fractions concrete and meaningful.",
      "evidence": "Part 1 - What happened: E2E Driver connected fractions to the familiar context of sharing a cake and students immediately related with personal anecdotes (a student mentioned a birthday cake). The teacher used this connection repeatedly to frame why we partition and why equal parts matter.\nPart 2 - Quote: \"When your mother brings a cake, do you eat it whole?\" \"No — we share it; we make equal parts.\""
    },
    {
      "title": "Warm, inclusive classroom climate",
      "impact": "Students were comfortable answering and participating; this foundation will support more student-centered activities when introduced.",
      "analysis": "A respectful tone and inclusion build trust and encourage participation, especially important in larger Pakistani classrooms where many students may hesitate to speak.",
      "evidence": "Part 1 - What happened: E2E Driver responded positively to student contributions, encouraged sharing, and used gentle redirection and praise. Students volunteered experiences and answered questions without visible anxiety.\nPart 2 - Quote: \"That's good — you brought cake for your brother's birthday?\" \"Yes.\""
    }
  ],
  "talk_time": {
    "analysis": "Teacher-dominated talk: E2E Driver explained concepts, asked rapid-fire questions, and provided examples for most of the 16-minute lesson. Student voice was present but limited to short chorused responses and a few individual answers; there were few extended student explanations or opportunities for independent practice during teacher circulation.",
    "student_percentage": 15,
    "teacher_percentage": 85
  },
